tching` supplies limited wildcard-name and exact origin/token ID matching with owner-scoped, undoable stop, and the GM Live FX manager now offers a separate scene-local, host-revalidated atomic stop by wildcard name or exact sequence/source/target ID (up to 16; stop requests from players are denied). A GM-reviewed Worker now has `fx.playAndWait` and a bounded `fx.sequence()` composition of sequential/parallel host-approved FX, finite waits, callbacks and reviewed nested-macro calls (≤32 sections, ≤8 parallel jobs, 7-second wait budget/finite cue deadline within a 10-second Worker). A production Chromium fixture authors a real saved cue and executes a reviewed sequence chain. **Not** a general Sequencer scheduler: no persistent await, client decode/finish acknowledgement, arbitrary nested sequences, transactional multi-section rollback, cancellation graph or extension API. **Delivery (SQ-13/A10)** is now partly closed: media a timeline will need is prefetched ahead of its cue according to each viewer's own 0–8000 ms window (`0` = lazy fetch at cue time), readiness is measured *before* the fetch (so a slow client is identified instead of hidden), a section more than 120 ms late either starts when its bytes land or is skipped according to that viewer's setting, and the run produces one report line — nothing at all when it arrived on time. A per-device panel (GM Settings and the player's guide) adds reduced camera motion (a pan cuts to its destination, a shake is skipped) and FX-sound muting that never affect anyone else; the OS `prefers-reduced-motion` switch only seeds a default a viewer can override. The host reports a partial audience to a GM requester via `fx.delivery` (`0x4d`, counts only: audience/rights/anchor/media, plus how many recipients received the run **without** a section targeted elsewhere and how many entitled viewers were left with none of it — a withheld section is not a skip), and the wizard warns before a save about registry gaps (asset absent, codec this browser refuses, GM-only media in a scene-audience timeline). **Sound lifecycle (SQ-09/SQ-17)** is now partly closed: a sound section names a **channel** (`sfx`/`music`/`ambience`/`voice`, host-validated as a closed set) and may carry **fades** bounded by its own duration; each device mixes those channels with its own gains (master mute plus four faders), the player re-evaluates gain on a short tick so a fade ramps and a fader moved mid-cue reaches the element, a silenced channel is **skipped without fetching its bytes** (the same `muted` reason as the master switch), a loop fades in once and holds, and the device panel lists what *this browser* is playing with a "Stop here" that leaves the host's instance untouched. Still required: the full chain/programmatic API, persistent lifecycle, the crosshair's shapes-committed-*as*-regions mode (a drag is offered for a visual section's start/destination, not for a camera look-at, a path waypoint or a summon) and the remaining on-canvas player modes, the camera's remaining shapes (targeting is by the audience — named users now exist (D-316), but there are no **groups**, no per-*token* targeting, a visual/sound section still reaches every recipient, and the delivery notice counts rather than naming who was targeted — plus per-leg durations and curved/looping legs), masks/elevation, positional/occluded audio and a dedicated host sound manager, effect masks (blend modes, one bounded filter or a 2–4 entry chain (D-313), host-resolved circle/cone/ray/rect masks with cutouts and hand-drawn polygon regions now exist; drawing a region **on the canvas** with the crosshair, a wall-bound polygon that is not star-shaped about its anchor, holes/rings inside a region, animation of tint/alpha, keyframes or multi-stop tracks, and masks on sound/camera/wait sections do not), extensible sections and measured synchronization/performance. Positional audio (D-309) covers distance, screen-space pan and host-answered muffling against the *